// @run
// @log a
// @log b
// GRADUATED from `error-litunion-list-arg-not-pinned.vl`, and it is the file that LICENCED the
// widening rather than a casualty of it.
//
// `nameIsI32Array` accepts FOUR spellings that all ride the one i32 list backing: `i32[]`,
// `boolean[]`, a litunion `K[]` and the `(boolean|null)[]` / `(K|null)[]` sentinel niches. Only
// `boolean[]` was claimed as a monomorphization pin, because only its divergence had been
// measured — the D-SHAPEFIELD precedent that an unreached widening is not licenced. This file
// existed to make declining the rest FAIL CLOSED: a `K[]` argument kept the `i32[]` pin, `T`
// bound to plain `i32`, and the element read hit the floor LOUDLY, so no wrong value escaped.
//
// THE DIVERGENCE IS NOW MEASURED, which is exactly what the decline was waiting for. The same
// `i32[]` pin over a litunion argument is what made `indexOf<T>(self: T[], needle: T)` declare
// `needle` an i32 and take the caller's string ref — `vl check` rc 0 and an invalid module (see
// `tests/cases/std/array-litunion-needle.vl`). So `K[]` joins `boolean[]` as a claimed pin, and
// this file inverts: its own header named the oracle — *"the ORACLE is the concrete twin, which
// prints `a`"* — and that is now what it prints.
//
// TWO CONSUMERS HAD TO MOVE, not one, and only running the file says so. Pinning `K[]` got the
// element READ right and left `print` unable to spell it (`print of a literal-union atom whose
// type carries no member texts`): the instance's `x[0]` node is SHARED with the generic body and
// records `T`, while the instance's parameter ANNOTATION names `K[]`. `litAtomMemberTy` is the
// resolver two other consumers already used for that, and the print widen is the third.
//
// THE SIBLING NICHE IS STILL DECLINED and still fails closed — `(boolean|null)[]` reaches its
// own floor on the same shape, and no divergence has been measured for it. The widening is
// litunion-only on purpose.

type K = "a" | "b"

function gtake<T>(x: T) { print(x[0]) }

function main() {
  const ks: K[] = ["a", "b"]
  gtake(ks)
  // The CONCRETE twin that was this file's oracle while it rejected — kept, so the generic and
  // the non-generic answers stay pinned side by side.
  print(ks[1])
}
main()
